<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Five components of placement</h2>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">1. Competitive distance</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">What is the brand confused with — and what does that confusion cost? Confusion is rarely an aesthetic problem and almost always a price problem: whoever stands next to a competitor that produces more cheaply is measured against that competitor's price. Distance is not created by claiming it, but by the brand being perceived in a different category.</p>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">2. Sales channel</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Manufacturer, distributor and end customer assess the same offer by different criteria. Purchasing asks about margin, turnover rate and terms. Retail asks about shelf space and gaps in the range. The end customer asks about something else again — or does not ask at all, but simply reaches for it. A placement that serves only one of these stages gets stuck there.</p>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">3. Pricing latitude</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The achievable price is not a result of the costing but of the position. A technically superior product that stands in the wrong frame of comparison is sold through discounts — and the sales force gets used to it. The correction does not start with the price list but with the question of what you are compared against.</p>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">4. Robustness in trademark law</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A placement that cannot be defended legally is not a placement — it is a risk with an expiry date. This applies particularly to private labels that deliberately move close to a manufacturer brand, and to any naming meant to work internationally. How far you may go belongs in the design, not in a check afterwards: knowing the limit while designing saves revisions and gains latitude.</p>

  <h3 class="h3-rule reveal" style="margin:2rem 0 1rem;">5. Findability</h3>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Today the competitive field includes search — and increasingly generative systems that reference, summarise and recommend brands without linking to them. A brand that does not appear in these answers does not appear for a growing share of research. That is not an SEO question but a question of how well a brand can be described in machine-readable form.</p>
</section>

